\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\columnwidth]{figures/fig1_daf_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Per-task death rate differential vs.\ performance gap ($n{=}11$ task types). The near-perfect correlation ($r = 0.97$) is a structural consequence of death dominance (DAF $\to 1 \Rightarrow r \to 1$), not an independent finding. The sole outlier (\texttt{find-non-living-thing}, diamond marker) has zero deaths; its $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +9.58$ arises entirely from non-death score differences.}
\label{fig:decomposition}
\end{figure}

We analyze 11 of ScienceWorld's 30 task types (245 episodes); the 19 excluded types lack actionable routing signal due to development contamination (5), taxonomic redundancy (7), or agent-floor performance (7). An anti-cherry-picking analysis confirms death dominance holds in 18 of these 19 types ($\overline{\Delta\text{DR}} = +38.2$\,pp $>$ included tasks' $+28.7$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:per-task}), with extended validation on representative excluded tasks in Appendix~\ref{app:extended-validation}.

\subsection{Death Avoidance Decomposition}
\label{sec:death-decomp}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/death_action_timeline.pdf}
\vspace{-2mm}
\caption{Action sequences for 6 representative death episodes. The fatal \texttt{focus on} (arrow) appears in final steps after long \texttt{connect} sequences (``connect loop $\to$ fatal focus'').}
\label{fig:death-timeline}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\scriptsize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2.5pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrccc@{}}
\toprule
Task & $n$ & $\Delta_\text{full}$ & D\%\textsubscript{int} & D\%\textsubscript{det} & D / I / N \\
\midrule
id-life-stages-1       & 20  & $+47.50$ & 55   & 10   & 9 / 0 / 11  \\
lifespan-long          & 25  & $+44.00$ & 56   & 16   & 11 / 1 / 13 \\
lifespan-l-then-s      & 20  & $+40.80$ & 45   & 10   & 8 / 1 / 11  \\
inclined-plane         & 20  & $+36.00$ & 55   & 20   & 7 / 0 / 13  \\
freeze                 & 20  & $+30.50$ & 55   & 25   & 6 / 0 / 14  \\
meas-melt-point        & 20  & $+30.05$ & 55   & 25   & 6 / 0 / 14  \\
id-life-stages-2       & 20  & $+30.00$ & 50   & 20   & 6 / 0 / 14  \\
find-animal            & 20  & $+26.65$ & 50   & 25   & 5 / 0 / 15  \\
grow-fruit             & 20  & $+25.10$ & 45   & 20   & 6 / 1 / 13  \\
chem-mix               & 20  & $+21.80$ & 45   & 25   & 6 / 2 / 12  \\
\midrule
find-non-living$^\dagger$ & 40 & $+9.58$ & 0  & 0    & 16 / 4 / 20 \\
\midrule
\textbf{All (11 tasks)} & \textbf{245} & $\mathbf{+29.60}$ & \textbf{42.9} & \textbf{16.3} & \textbf{86 / 9 / 150} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Per-task decomposition. D\%: death rate for internal/deterministic. D/I/N: det-preferred / int-preferred / no-preference labels. $\dagger$\,Only task with zero deaths; $n{=}40$ (two batches).}
\label{tab:per-task}
\end{table}

Of the 95 non-tie routing labels (Figure~\ref{fig:label-decomposition}), 75 (78.9\%; 95\% CI: $[70.7\%, 87.0\%]$, episode-level bootstrap) arise from death asymmetry: the internal strategy triggers a catastrophic $-100$ penalty while the deterministic strategy survives. This penalty-independent label fraction is the primary diagnostic metric. Of these, 54 are sole-signal labels where death was the only discriminator; at $P{=}0$ these reduce to ties, but the labeling function itself is penalty-independent (Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling}).

DAF $= 90.4\%$ at $P{=}100$ ($\Delta_{\text{full}} = +29.60$, $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.84$; 95\% CI$_{\text{DAF}}$: $[81.3\%, 99.1\%]$, episode-level bootstrap). The 78.9\% label fraction provides the more robust, penalty-invariant characterization (\S\ref{sec:daf}).

\paragraph{Per-task structure.}
The internal strategy dies in 42.9\% of episodes versus 16.3\% for deterministic; all 105 internal deaths are triggered by the \texttt{focus on} action (Appendix~\ref{app:failure-modes}). Per-task $\Delta$DR correlates with $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ at $r = 0.97$ (Figure~\ref{fig:decomposition}; leave-one-out and bootstrap robustness in Appendix~\ref{app:correlation}), a structural consequence of DAF$\to 1$ rather than an independent finding. Figure~\ref{fig:death-timeline} illustrates the typical death trajectory.

\paragraph{Binary structure and robustness.}
All 10 death-prone task types produce 100\% death-asymmetry labels; only \texttt{find-non-living-thing} (zero deaths) produces non-death signal (SCI $= 0.21$; Appendix~\ref{app:sci}; case study in Appendix~\ref{app:non-death-case-study}). Leave-one-task-out analysis (Table~\ref{tab:loto-daf}) confirms robustness: removing any single death-prone task changes DAF by at most 3.0~pp. Bootstrap 95\% CIs: DAF $[81.3\%, 99.1\%]$; cluster-robust $[73.3\%, 100.0\%]$ ($K{=}11$; Appendix~\ref{app:wild-cluster}). Two independent replications (Gemma-2-9B-it, DAF${=}89.7\%$; seed=123, DAF${=}89.7\%$) converge within 0.7~pp.

\subsection{Multi-Model Validation (7--9B Tier)}
\label{sec:cross-model}

Llama-3.1-8B~\citep{llama3-2024} ($N{=}220$) and Gemma-2-9B-it~\citep{gemma2-2024} ($N{=}220$) independently replicate death dominance: DAF $= 88.2\%$ and $89.7\%$ respectively, with direction consistency 10/10 on death-prone tasks (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model}; per-task CIs across all 30 tasks in Appendix~\ref{app:per-task-ci}). Three architecturally distinct model families, Qwen (Alibaba), Llama (Meta), and Gemma (Google), converge on DAF $88$--$90\%$ with a spread of only 2.2~pp, despite differences in pretraining data, architecture, and tokenization. This narrow convergence suggests that death dominance reflects an environment-level property rather than a model-specific artifact. Scaling $10{\times}$ to two 70B-class models (\S\ref{sec:agent-improvement}) yields DAF${=}92.4$--$101.0\%$, indicating that the pattern persists---and may intensify---at larger scale.

